// Go's `slices`: generic functions on slices, under Go's names.
//
// THE ALGEBRA, BEFORE THE NAMES. A slice here is a table, a function on
// [0, len). EqualFunc decides whether two tables are equal under a relation
// eq: len(a) = len(b) ∧ ∀i < len(a). eq(a[i], b[i]).
//
// ITS CALLS ARE A SEQUENCE ITS ARGUMENTS DETERMINE (ADR 0143), and Go's
// documentation says so: "the elements are compared in increasing index
// order, and the comparison stops at the first index for which eq returns
// false". So the activations are eq(a[0], b[0]), eq(a[1], b[1]), …, up to and
// including the first that answers false, and none when the lengths differ:
// for equal lengths n, their number is min(lcp + 1, n), lcp the length of the
// longest prefix eq relates. That is `ordered`, and a λ given to it may write
// a cell of its caller's, threaded in that order (Theorem T₁).
module go/slices {
    set IntEq = fn(x ∈ Int, y ∈ Int) (b ∈ Bool) ordered
    fn EqualFunc(a ∈ Array(Int), b ∈ Array(Int), eq ∈ IntEq) (Bool) pure {
        host expr "slices.EqualFunc(${a}, ${b}, ${eq})" import "slices"
    }
}
